{"reference":"Acts 18","book":"Acts","chapter":18,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Acts presents the Corinthian work as grounded in ordinary labor, synagogue discussion, hospitality, and prolonged teaching. Paul works alongside Aquila and Priscilla, reasons each Sabbath in the synagogue, and then teaches in the adjacent house of Titius Justus after opposition arises. The movement between these settings helps explain the chapter’s mission as collaborative and situated in several connected places, rather than as a placeless preaching episode or a fixed formula for forming communities.","verse_refs":["Acts 18:1-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_ffbb0451a3162e4d02fb2826"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The final movement continues this pattern of connected people and places. In Ephesus, Priscilla and Aquila privately explain the way of God more accurately to Apollos; the believers then commend him as he travels to Achaia, where he publicly argues from the Scriptures. The account depicts instruction, correction, recommendation, and travel as shared work, but it does not supply a complete institutional hierarchy or biography for the people involved.","verse_refs":["Acts 18:18-28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_962b1b8958e3d5e88311db6b"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Rome and Corinth","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Aquila and Priscilla are said to have recently come from Italy because Claudius ordered Jews to leave Rome. Other ancient reports concern Claudian action affecting Jews or Jewish meetings there, but the scope, enforcement, timing, and the meaning of the name “Chrestus” in one report remain disputed. The verse therefore explains the couple’s displacement without proving that every Jew left Rome or that the order was certainly caused by conflict over Christ.","verse_refs":["Acts 18:1-3","Acts 18:24-28","Acts 18:3"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_77144a321ef32a6987bfad0f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Gallio’s appearance before the tribunal is not merely a narrative detail. An imperial inscription from Delphi independently identifies Gallio as proconsul of Achaia, providing a chronological anchor close to this Corinthian setting, often placed around 51–52 CE. The inscription does not mention Paul, the hearing, or Sosthenes, so it supports Gallio’s office without independently confirming every detail of Acts’ scene.","verse_refs":["Acts 18:12-17","Acts 18:18-26","Acts 18:8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_be4bf180014544e8310cac4a"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Travel Connections","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Corinth stood on an isthmus between Cenchreae on the eastern side and Lechaeum on the western side, linking sea routes with north-south land travel. This makes the departure from Cenchreae in verse 18 more concrete: the city’s setting connected it to wider travel networks. Ephesus likewise had harbor infrastructure linking it to the Aegean and inland Anatolia, helping explain the chapter’s repeated arrivals, departures, and movement of coworkers without requiring a particular ship, route, cargo, or duration.","verse_refs":["Acts 18:1-5","Acts 18:12-17","Acts 18:18-21"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_ceaa0a3c944dab96006ca3d4"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions Worth Holding Open","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Acts gives Prisca and Aquila shared agency in instructing Apollos. That supports taking Prisca’s theological teaching seriously, while the chapter does not identify her with a modern seminary category or ordained office. More broadly, the descriptions attached to people in these texts—teaching, hosting, coworking, service, and household activity—should not automatically be treated as identical roles or arranged into a fixed hierarchy.","verse_refs":["Acts 18:1-3","Acts 18:1-5","Acts 18:18-26","Acts 18:24-28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_6c04f83910b81219c06c0f59"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"Sosthenes, the synagogue ruler beaten before Gallio’s tribunal, may be the same Sosthenes named as a co-sender in 1 Corinthians. The shared name alone, however, cannot establish that identification.","verse_refs":["Acts 18:17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_9ccd3379bdbb12ca8889c5cc"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Acts Alongside Other Sources","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"Acts is preserved in early papyri and major Greek manuscripts, including P45, P53, P74, Sinaiticus, Vaticanus, and Bezae. These witnesses preserve different portions and textual profiles; Bezae and related witnesses are notably distinctive in Acts, so individual readings require case-by-case judgment.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_dbe992fe120c98b9571807f1","reader_path_acts_018_cc20f40eae418f10645940a2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_9","text":"Acts and Paul’s letters are distinct witnesses to the travel setting around Athens, Macedonia, Corinth, and Timothy. Their overlapping references can illuminate the setting, but a seamless itinerary is a reconstruction rather than the wording of either source. For chronology, the letters should be considered on their own terms rather than having Acts’ sequence silently replace their plans, emphases, or silences.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_018_d88975bb7ed158c8b1f5712a","reader_path_acts_018_5bc51cc160d15cd81c2089a3"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}